e set without the half). If one half's complement fails, minimize that half; else if both halves' complements pass, then we try to reduce by removing individual elements, etc. The standard algorithm is described in [Zeller's delta debugging paper].\n\nGiven the small size (8 items), we can implement a recursive binary search.\n\nBut we must be careful: the algorithm typically works on a list of changes, and the test function is on sets. We'll implement a simple version.\n\nImplementation outline:\n- Let full = set(items) (or maybe the list of items? We need to consider ordering? The algorithm usually takes a list. We'll treat items as an ordered list.\n- Define test_function(set_of_items): returns 'FAIL', 'PASS', 'UNRESOLVED'.\n- Use ddmin recursive function that takes a list of changes (the current candidate list) and returns (min_list, tests).\n- Base case: if len(list) == 1, return that list.\n- Otherwise, split into two halves. For each half, create a set that is the full set minus that half (i.e., the complement). Test the complement.\n- If the complement of one half fails, recursively minimize that half (i.e., the current list becomes that half).\n- If both complements fail, it's tricky; standard algorithm says then you can take the first half and continue.\n- If neither complements fail, then you try to remove each element individually (granularity 1). For each element, test the set without that element. If removal yields failure, drop that element and continue with reduced set; else keep it.\n- Return the minimal list and count tests.\n\nWe'll need to count each call to test_function.\n\nWe then output:\n- minimal_failure: list of items from the minimal set, sorted? Not specified. But the fixtures likely expect deterministic order. The input items are ordered. We can return in the order they appear in the minimal list (the order of the list used in ddmin). Or we can output in the same order as items? The schema doesn't enforce order, but for consistency, we can sort alphabetically (since items are strings like \"d0\", \"d1\", etc.) or maintain original order. Let's sort for predictability.\n\n- outcome: \"FAIL\" if test(minimal_set) == \"FAIL\" else appropriately.\n- tests: total count of test calls.\n\nThis approach should produce meaningful results that match typical delta debugging output.\n\nNow, we need to implement solve(case) that returns the dict.\n\nWe'll test on the given fixtures manually to see plausible outputs:\nCase 0: items ['d0'..'d7'], required ['d2','d3'], unresolved [].\nFull set test: required present, unresolved empty -> FAIL.\nddmin: start with full list. Split into [d0,d1,d2,d3] and [d4,d5,d6,d7]. Complement of first half = [d4,d5,d6,d7] -> test: required not present -> PASS. Complement of second half = [d0,d1,d2,d3] -> test: required present, no unresolved -> FAIL. So we recursively minimize first half (the failing complement's opposite? Actually we minimize the half whose complement fails, i.e., the first half? Wait: complement of second half fails, so the second half is the one to minimize? Standard: if complement of A fails, then minimize A (the removed set). So here complement of second half fails -> minimize second half? Let's step carefully:\nWe have set S = full. Split into two parts: A and B. Test S \\ A (which is B) and S \\ B (which is A). If S \\ A fails, then we minimize A because removing A still fails, so failure depends on A. If S \\ B fails, minimize B.\nIn this case, S = full. A = [d0,d1,d2,d3], B = [d4,d5,d6,d7]. \nS \\ A = B -> test(B) = PASS (no required items). S \\ B = A -> test(A) = FAIL (required items present). So S \\ B fails, thus minimize B? Wait S \\ B = A fails, so the failure is caused by items in A (since removing B still fails). Actually, if S \\ B fails, that means removing B (keeping A) still fails, so the failure is contained in A. So we should minimize A (the set that when kept causes failure). The standard algorithm: if test(S \\ A) fails, minimize A; if test(S \\ B) fails, minimize B. Here test(A) fails, which is S \\ B, so minimize B? Let's re-check notation: Usually ddmin takes a list of changes, and consider removing each half. If removing the first half (i.e., test on second half) fails, then the first half is irrelevant, minimize second half. Actually typical ddmin algorithm:\ndef ddmin(c):\n    n = len(c)\n    if n == 1:\n        return c\n    split into two halves c1 and c2\n    if test(c - c1) == FAIL:\n        return ddmin(c1)\n    elif test(c - c2) == FAIL:\n        return ddmin(c2)\n    else:\n        # try to reduce further by removing individual elements\n        ...\nSo they test c - c1 (i.e., keep c2) and c - c2 (keep c1). If keeping c2 fails, then failure depends on c1, so reduce to c1? Actually if test(c - c1) fails, that means removing c1 leaves c2, and test(c2) fails, so the failure is in c2, so we can discard c1 and minimize c2. Wait, they recursively call ddmin(c1) or ddmin(c2)? In the code snippet, they call ddmin(c1) if test(c - c1) fails, and ddmin(c2) if test(c - c2) fails. This suggests that if the complement of c1 fails, then c1 is responsible, so minimize c1. Let's test: c - c1 = c2. If test(c2) fails, then failure is present without c1, so c1 is not needed. That would lead to minimizing c2, not c1. So the snippet seems reversed? I need to recall exactly.\n\nBetter to refer to Zeller's algorithm:\n- ddmin(C) where C is a list of changes.\n- Let n = |C|, if n==1 return C.\n- Let n1 = n//2, n2 = n - n1.\n- Let C1 = first n1 changes, C2 = remaining n2.\n- If test(C \\ C1) == FAIL: return ddmin(C2)  (because C \\ C1 = C2 fails, so failure is due to C2)\n- Else if test(C \\ C2) == FAIL: return ddmin(C1)\n- Else: try to reduce by removing single elements in increasing granularity.\n\nActually, I think the correct logic: If the test without C1 (i.e., C2) fails, then the failure is caused by C2, so we can discard C1 and minimize C2. So ddmin(C2). Similarly, if test without C2 fails, minimize C1. That makes sense.\nSo we'll implement that: \n- if test(set_of_C2) == FAIL: minimize C2 (the kept set).\n- elif test(set_of_C1) == FAIL: minimize C1.\n- else: perform reduction by individual removal.\n\nWe'll need to be careful with the meaning of test: test expects a set of items that are present. So we pass the set of items in the complement.\n\nNow, for case 0, full set S. C1 = [d0,d1,d2,d3], C2 = [d4,d5,d6,d7]. test(C2) = PASS, test(C1) = FAIL. So we minimize C1 (since test without C2 fails, i.e., test(C1) fails). So we recursively ddmin on C1 = [d0,d1,d2,d3].\nNow on C1, split: C1a=[d0,d1], C1b=[d2,d3]. test(C1a) = PASS? required not present, so PASS. test(C1b) = FAIL? required present: [d2,d3] -> FAIL. So test(C1b) fails -> minimize C1b (since test without C1a = C1b fails). So we recursive on [d2,d3]. Now size 2, split: [d2], [d3]. test(full complement of [d2]? Wait, we are now in sublist [d2,d3]. S' = {d2,d3}. Split: C1=[d2], C2=[d3]. test(C2) = test({d3}) = PASS (since only d3, missing d2 -> required not all present -> PASS. test(C1) = test({d2}) = PASS (missing d3). So neither complement fails (both PASS). Then we go to individual removal. For each element, test set without that element. For element d2: test({d3}) = PASS -> removal fails (i.e., test returns PASS, not FAIL), so we keep d2. For element d3: test({d2}) = PASS, keep d3. So we cannot reduce further. Minimal set = [d2,d3]. The number of tests: let's count:\n- Initial full set: test(full) = FAIL (1 test)\n- Then test(C2) and test(C1) on full: 2 tests -> total 3.\n- On C1: test(C1a), test(C1b): 2 tests -> total 5.\n- On C1b: test(C2 of that set), test(C1): 2 tests -> total 7.\n- Individual removal: test({d3}), test({d2}): 2 tests -> total 9.\nSo tests = 9. That seems plausible. But is that the expected? Possibly the algorithm counts only tests that return FAIL? Or all tests? Usually all calls.\n\nNow for case 1 with unresolved_without, the test function includes unresolved check. We'll need to implem